WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.680
When the Babylon Bee reported that
Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.180
had appeared on The Price is Right, guessing that everything presented by host Drew Carey,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.640
including world-class health care, costs nothing at all,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.360
the prominent online fact-checking site, Snopes, assured readers that this didn't actually occur.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.120
It labeled the story false, along with,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:26.380
"Democrats demand that  Brett Kavanaugh submit to DNA tests to prove he's not actually Hitler."

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.240
and "CNN purchases industrial sized washing machine to spin news before publication."

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:38.600
And they fact check and say no, you know, we checked the record. CNN didn't buy a washing machine recently.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.160
And so that was kind of bizarre. It does seem like they fact-check our ones that  target the left more often.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.220
Kyle Mann is the editor-in-chief of the Christian satirical news site which has surged in popularity,

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.720
boasting about 10 million monthly page views just three years after its founding.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:59.720
We don't necessarily target one side over the other, and we don't necessarily strive for balance.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.600
We look to target whoever's being ridiculous, you know, on a given day.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.440
There's plenty to go around. And there's plenty to go around on both sides.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.580
Snopes defense for fact-checking satire is that a lot of people are misled by it,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.780
and that they're  just trying to help people strengthen their media literacy skills.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.340
Do you see that as legitimate and has that been consistent with your experience with Snopes?

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:29.460
We don't necessarily disagree with the idea of someone saying hey we're gonna fact-check a joke, you know,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.780
or we're gonna fact-check this piece.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.220
Because yeah sometimes there's a lot of people out there that don't recognize satire.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.480
The real concern is that when we start looking at, you know, who is the fact checker?

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.880
Like who are these people that are supposed to be these objective fact checkers?

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.740
You know, and it's really concerning when someone sets themselves up and they say

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.360
whenever we say something is true you know we slap this green true label on it,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.060
or we say it's false and we slapped this red false label.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.960
And how do we know that that's accurate? You know, we've seen  ideological bias from Snopes in the past.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.021
After Georgia lawmaker, Erika Thomas, retracted her claim

00:02:04.021 --> 00:02:09.600
that someone had told her to go back to her country, the Bee published an article poking fun at the incident.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.840
Again Snopes accused Mann and his colleagues of publishing fake news.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.520
We even made a joke saying that she
went to Chick-Fil-A,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:22.120
and the Chick-Fil-A worker says, "my pleasure" you know and she says, oh he told me to go back to my country.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.822
We're known for our Chick-Fil-A jokes like that's one of our main things that we go back to.

00:02:26.822 --> 00:02:33.120
They filed us under the category junk nudes, and said that we were trying to fan the flames of controversy.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.420
And the ironic thing was they didn't even fact-check Erica Thomas's original claim,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.120
but they fact checked I joke about it.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.380
So that's when we start to be concerned about some kind of slant or bias.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.280
Mann says that by mischaracterizing the Bee's content,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.520
Snopes could have a negative impact on its traffic and revenue.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.400
We've been threatened with demonetization and deplatforming from Facebook,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.020
when they had a relationship with Snopes for fact checking.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:01.420
So that's kind of concerning, when a lot of these social media sites are looking at how to squash fake news

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.560
and if we start to get lumped in with sites that are actually sharing fake news,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.580
then it could be a big problem for
our platform and how we reach people.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.620
Snopes, which didn't respond to Reasons
interview requests,

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.920
has said that a significant percentage of social media users mistake satire for real news.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.560
But Mann says that when making this claim, Snope cited a methodologically flawed study,

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.660
In which participants were showed Babylon Bee headlines reworded and taken out of context.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.760
A lot of times they missed the joke entirely.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.140
They didn't even accurately represent what our joke was trying to say,

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.540
and just did a very literal interpretation of it,

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.020
and in that context I can understand why a lot of people were deceived.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.220
Although the Babylon Bee is primarily concerned with making its readers laugh

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.680
man says that the site has a more serious mission too: uncovering truth

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.500
You can take satire and you can use it to divide

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.880
you can cut away what we believe is true or what believe is important from what's not important

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.500
and we see that in satire of politics, we see that in satire of of religion.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:05.520
You know specifically when we when we do satire about the modern church we do satire about Christian living,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.180
you know we can take a lot of these these sacred cows
that Christians have set up and just skewer 'em.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.620
You know and obviously a lot
of people don't appreciate that

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:17.960
but we feel like it's a great weapon, it's a 
 great tool to kind of separate what's important from what's not

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.660
whether that's politics or our Christianity.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.360
Mann says that while Snopes is wrong to treat the
Babylon be as a serious news source

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.470
the satirical site does seek to help readers
better understand the real world.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:34.800
I'd like to say that the satire is not truth
but it's like truth adjacent

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.180
so you're creating this alternate world you're
creating this alternate reality.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.640
It's almost a mirror that holds up this
reflection of our world.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:49.580
And we can help draw people's world views and opinions
to a logical conclusion.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.900
And then some people can look at a look at that and
maybe say 'whoa' you know,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.340
'maybe my ideas do need to be thought out a little bit more'

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.700
and sometimes not, you know, sometimes people will just get a good laugh out of it

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.500
and that's ok with us too.

